(p-321) Surah "Ṣād"

Its purpose is to clarify what was mentioned at the end of "al-Ṣāffāt" (The Rangers): that the soldiers of Allah are the victorious, even if they appear weak and their victory is delayed. The final outcome is triumph, and it brings safety to both groups, because He, glory be to Him, is One, since He encompasses the attributes of perfection. This is what the end of "al-Ṣāffāt" conveyed, through the declaration of His transcendence (tanzīh), praise (ḥamd), and what accompanies them. The naming of the surah after the letter "ṣ" (Ṣād) also indicates this, because its point of articulation (makhraj) is from the tip of the tongue and between the roots of the two lower incisors. It has these qualities (ṣifāt): whisper (hams), softness (rakhāwa), closure (iṭbāq), elevation (istiʿlāʾ), and whistling (ṣafīr). It therefore signifies this meaning, because its point of articulation is among the most accommodating, widest, lightest, most graceful, and most prevalent of the points of articulation of the letters. Also, the lofty qualities it possesses are more numerous than their opposites, and more majestic, higher, and grander. For this reason, the prophets mentioned in it are those through whose hands no destruction took place; rather, they were tested and made known, and Allah protected them from their enemies among the jinn and mankind. The allusion to this is found in what has been narrated from Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, and from others: that its meaning is "Allah is truthful in what He promised," or "Muḥammad (ﷺ) spoke the truth," or "Muḥammad (ﷺ) hunted the hearts of creation and won them over." Abū ʿAmr [ibn al-ʿAlāʾ] recited it, in a rare (shādh) narration, as a past-tense verb from "ṣād" (to hunt). Al-Ḥasan and others recited it with a kasra on the ṣād, as an imperative from "muṣādāh" (confrontation), which means opposition. That is: confront the creatures with what We have sent down to you and argue with them through it, for you will overcome them, because truth is the sword of Allah on His earth; it never strikes anything without cutting it. This truth, which the letter ṣād points to, has spread over every truth in existence, and so it wins over everyone who has some share of truth. For this reason, He said in the surah after it: ﴿And he who brought the truth and believed in it﴾ [al-Zumar: 33]. The mention of these prophets, peace be upon them, is an existential witness to what the letter ṣād means according to the godly scholars (al-ʿulamāʾ al-rabbāniyyūn): that it is the correspondence between creation and command. It is also called "The Surah of Dāwūd," peace be upon him, as Ibn al-Jawzī, may Allah have mercy on him, said. The state of the Prophet (ﷺ) is the clearest indication, among the states of those mentioned in it of the prophets, of this purpose, because he was weak at first and then attained dominion at the end. "In the name of Allah," who honors whoever belongs to Him, even if he is weak, because He is the Mighty. "The Most Merciful," who has complete power to show mercy in adversity as He shows mercy in prosperity. "The Most Compassionate," who honors the people of His love by helping them to persist in gratitude and praise to Him.

When our Lord, glory be to Him, declared His most holy Self free from every flaw of deficiency at the end of that surah, and affirmed for Himself every perfection, explicitly stating His might, and made it obligatory for the messengers to be safe, He opened this surah with an allusion to the proof of that, namely the humiliation of whoever disputes it. So He said: ﴿Ṣād﴾. That is: Your matter, O you whom We commanded to seek the verdict of the disobedient at the end of "al-Ṣāffāt," and whom We gave the good news of victory, is prepared, despite the weakness you are in now, and despite softness, closure, and an elevation and spread that will fill the horizons. ﴿By the Qur'an﴾: that is, the one that gathers, together with clarity, every good, for followers who cannot be counted and whom no limit can contain. Since an oath is not fitting, nor does it be appropriate, except by that which the one sworn to believes to be noble, He said: ﴿Possessor of the Reminder﴾: that is, of admonition and remembrance through what is recognized, of loftiness, nobility, and truth, which is beyond doubt for everyone. So whoever hears it believes in its nobility and confirms the one who brought it. Its nobility, revealed upon him, will certainly fill the regions and will certainly exceed every measure, as the indication of this was already given by the first letter. And those who disbelieve, although they display doubt about this and disparage it in speech, they do not disparage it in knowledge;
